From 11580e3800d3ee4e532f3589a2854e5a16e30320 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 10:29:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wlcsm: work around the Broadcom netlink port-allocation
 defect in libnvram.so

Field incident, caught live on metal 2026-08-11 and then confirmed instruction
by instruction in the shipped binary: an nvram save wedged permanently while the
router stayed up and SSH-accessible. strace showed it creating a netlink socket,
failing bind() with EADDRINUSE for PID..PID+9, printing "_wlcsm_init failed!",
and immediately repeating - forever.

ROOT CAUSE (libnvram.so, disassembly-confirmed)

_wlcsm_init() stores getpid() into wlcsm_agent+0x18 and then hands the ADDRESS
OF THAT SAME FIELD to the internal netlink allocator as its port-candidate
out-parameter:

    3bbc:  str r0, [r1, #24]!   ; saved_pid = getpid(); r1 = &saved_pid
    3bc4:  bl  3930             ; alloc(&agent, &saved_pid, 0)

The allocator binds NETLINK protocol 31 with nl_pid = *candidate and, on
EADDRINUSE, walks ten candidates (0x396c: mov r7,#10) writing each one back
through that pointer:

    39b8:  add r6, r6, #1
    39bc:  str r6, [r5]         ; mutates the caller's saved_pid

So the candidate port and the saved PID are the same storage. After any
collision saved_pid != getpid(), and the next entry tests exactly that:

    3c30:  cmp r4, r0           ; saved_pid vs getpid()
    3c34:  bne 3ba8             ; "the PID changed" -> re-init

The re-init path never close()s the previous fd. Each pass therefore leaks one
netlink socket and drifts the candidate further, until all ten are burned and
_wlcsm_init() fails for good. wlcsm_nvram_getall() then retries the failing send
without bound (5b2c: bne 5b0c) while holding g_WLCSM_NVRAM_MUTEX, and because
_wlcsm_netlink_send_mesg() re-enters _wlcsm_init() (3e4c), every iteration redoes
the whole socket + ten-bind dance: ~16 syscalls per pass with no backoff. That
burns a core in system time, floods stderr, and the nvram write never completes.
The leaked ports then squat for the life of the process, making the next
PID-reuse collision more likely - so it compounds with uptime and process churn.

Trigger: PID reuse landing on a netlink port a long-lived process already owns
(here, debug_monitor holding 4986/4987 when an nvram save was later given PID
4986). Killing the port holder released it with no reboot.

THE WORKAROUND

Every step of that chain is gated on the FIRST bind failing. If it succeeds, the
allocator never increments, saved_pid stays == getpid(), the false "PID changed"
branch never fires, nothing leaks and nothing spins. wlcsm_bindfix.so interposes
bind() and, only when the real call already returned EADDRINUSE on an AF_NETLINK
protocol-31 socket with a non-zero nl_pid, retries once with nl_pid = 0 so the
kernel autobinds a free port. Everything else passes through untouched.

This is not an exotic change to the subsystem: Broadcom's own SECOND WLCSM socket
is already allocated with the candidate seeded to zero (3c60: str r4,[sp] with
r4=0), i.e. it already uses kernel autobind.

The closed library is not modified. This is our own object loaded beside it.

DEFAULT OFF. /etc/ld.so.preload is global to every dynamically linked process and
this has not yet run on hardware - the same risk class as the /proc readers behind
the accel-experiment reboot loops. Ships inert; enable deliberately with
  nvram set wlcsm_bindfix=1 && nvram commit && reboot
Back to 0 removes the preload on the next boot. Per-process opt-out without a
reboot: WLCSM_BINDFIX=0 in that process's environment.

Residual risk to settle on metal: after a rescue the socket's port no longer
equals the PID. Nothing in the kernel cares; the open question is whether the
wlcsm daemon ever addresses a process by a port it assumes equals its PID. Every
rescue emits a syslog line naming the pid and port, so it is visible if it bites.

Note for a vendor report: the library's error path writes to stderr via fprintf
despite linking logmessage_normal, so on a router these failures never reach
syslog - which is much of why this has gone unexplained for years.

Built and behaviour-tested off-target: cross-compiles clean for elf32-littlearm
(7,520 bytes, links only libdl/libc/ld-linux - deliberately NOT libnvram, which it
wraps); rescue path, kill switch, protocol scoping and non-netlink passthrough all
verified.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
